summary:
  The spacing between two members' plays in the shared capture goes back to
  1.8 s, which is not a whole number of graph cycles.

history:
  Each member is its own paplay, and a stream starts at the next graph cycle
  after it is asked for, so its start latency is a sawtooth of the phase the
  request lands in. Two plays a slot apart share that latency only when the
  slot is whole cycles. 1.8 s is 168.75 cycles of a 512-frame graph and
  84.375 of a 1024-frame one: a quarter, or three eighths, of all rounds start
  the second member a cycle off, and the median of three passes that on as the
  two clusters one quantum apart first written down on 2026-08-12 (33 %
  measured, 31.6 % predicted). Measured 2026-09-21, thirty rounds each and
  interleaved: a third of the rounds jumped at 1.8 s, a fifth at 1.792 s, and
  the constant 2 ms the old spacing carried went with it.

target:
  package/contents/ui/calibrate.py

find:
  ULTRA_SLOT_SECONDS = 1.792 # how much of ONE shared capture each member owns.

replace:
  ULTRA_SLOT_SECONDS = 1.8   # how much of ONE shared capture each member owns.

expect:
  Killed by tests/test_calibrate.py::test_two_members_one_slot_apart_start_with_the_same_latency.
  Line-neutral.
